WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.320
Hey, this is super interesting.&nbsp;
Last week we did a piece about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.920
15 dollar minimum wage, which which I support.
You know, I've pointed out there are parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:19.520
the country where 15 is almost or even more than&nbsp;
a doubling of the current minimum wage. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:25.840
may not be the cost of living adjusted wage, but&nbsp;
big picture, I support a 15 dollar minimum wage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:32.080
And we talked about how this new radical, wacky&nbsp;
Republican Congresswoman Lauren Bobert tweeted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.400
about the minimum wage, something really dumb.&nbsp;
She tweeted, quote, I got my start working at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.520
McDonalds. That job doesn't exist. If the minimum&nbsp;
wage is raised to fifteen dollars per hour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.800
small business would be crushed by this arbitrary&nbsp;
mandate and big business would simply automate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:53.200
away jobs. Not everything that sounds good is&nbsp;
actually good in practice. And what is fascinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.640
is that now it just so happens that the companies&nbsp;
she mentioned, McDonalds, has put out a statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:05.680
about this. McDonald's CEO Chris Kaminsky did&nbsp;
a fourth quarter earnings call with investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.560
on Thursday after we had covered this story.&nbsp;
And he said the company is doing just fine in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.080
states that have increased minimum wages beyond&nbsp;
the federal minimum wage and we are basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.600
going to be fine. We will do just fine even if&nbsp;
minimum wages continue increasing in a variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:30.080
different states. Now, Kamiński wasn't this was a&nbsp;
call with investors. The point here is to reassure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.520
investors it's not that comes Linskey wants&nbsp;
higher minimum wages. It's not that McDonald's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.080
is now on for the fight for 15 or this is merely&nbsp;
a CEO talking to investors and saying, listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:47.360
twenty nine states already have higher wages&nbsp;
than the federal minimum and we're doing just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:55.600
fine. And this is what a four year analysis by&nbsp;
a team of economists found for McDonald's that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.960
in applicable markets where wages have gone&nbsp;
up. And as I talked to you about before, D.C.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:07.600
is already 15 bucks an hour. McDonald's is doing&nbsp;
fine. California's 13 to 14 an hour. McDonald's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:14.800
is doing fine in California. Washington state is&nbsp;
I believe it's 13 nine. McDonald's is doing fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:21.680
in Washington state and on and on and on. He was&nbsp;
just reassuring investors of what we already know.&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.360
And it is true it is true that&nbsp;
when the minimum wage goes up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:35.760
the cost of products go up. But this is them. This&nbsp;
is such a key finding. The study found that when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:42.960
the the minimum wage goes up 10 percent, the cost&nbsp;
of a of a Big Mac goes up one point four percent.&nbsp;

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.360
Why would it be that if you have a bigger increase&nbsp;
to the wage, the cost of the food only goes up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:56.960
tiny bit? It's because just as I said last week,&nbsp;
labor is only a fraction of the total cost. You've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:04.960
got rent for these really expensive locations that&nbsp;
McDonald's often operate out of. You have the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:11.280
of all of the capital. You have the cost of the&nbsp;
food itself. You have insurance, you have all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:18.480
these different things. And yes, you have labor.&nbsp;
And what we again learn is that labor is only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.960
one input and you can do wage increases that are&nbsp;
meaningful to the employees. And they have only a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:31.600
minor impact on the cost of a big of a Big Mac.
Now, clueless people like Lauren Bobert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:38.000
have been able to scam their way into positions&nbsp;
of legislative power by attaching themselves to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:44.480
really bad ideas, not based in science. Like&nbsp;
if the minimum wage goes from eight to ten,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:50.080
then that's 25 percent. That means the food&nbsp;
will all cost twenty five percent more and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.240
suddenly tons of people can't afford the&nbsp;
food. That's not the reality. The reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.080
is that meaningful minimum wage increases will&nbsp;
have a tiny effect on the food, which, yes, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.800
food will be a tiny bit more expensive. But way&nbsp;
more employees now have money with which they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:14.720
can go to other local businesses and buy things.&nbsp;
That's called demand side stimulus. So nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.400
of what I'm telling you today is new.
We did this analysis last week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.920
but hearing it straight from the horse's&nbsp;
mouth, the McDonald's CEO saying we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:29.280
done the analysis. We have twenty nine states&nbsp;
where minimum wages have gone up. It's fine.&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:35.280
It's fine. And it leads to this tiny, tiny&nbsp;
increase to the cost of a Big Mac. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:39.200
I know that there will be the thousand&nbsp;
dollar an hour troll's, as I call them.&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:46.160
But but, David, if fifteen dollars is better&nbsp;
than eight, why not pay everybody a hundred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:52.160
dollars an hour? If more money is good,&nbsp;
pay everybody a thousand dollars an hour.&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:58.080
We'll know a thousand dollars an hour is not&nbsp;
a cost of living equilibrium. Minimum wage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:04.320
A thousand dollars an hour is not an industry&nbsp;
balanced minimum wage. There is no metric that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:10.400
justifies a thousand dollars an hour for people&nbsp;
working at McDonald's. It is not a productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:15.920
adjusted wage. It is not an inflation adjusted&nbsp;
wage. That would not make any sense. If you made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:21.680
the minimum wage at McDonald's a thousand dollars&nbsp;
an hour, you would have doctors in many cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.320
saying I'm better off working at McDonald's. No,&nbsp;
nobody's saying that. We're talking about fifteen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:33.440
Some more expensive places in the country would&nbsp;
justify, you know, eighteen, twenty one in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.560
York City or whatever. Nobody's talking about&nbsp;
a thousand. That's called reductio ad absurdum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:45.680
It's a slippery slope. It's a red herring.&nbsp;
It's a lot of different things. So again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.080
a lot of what I'm saying here, we said&nbsp;
last week, but now even the McDonald's CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:55.680
when the CEO reassures investors we're&nbsp;
doing fine with higher minimum wages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.320
you know, that they're doing fine with higher&nbsp;
minimum wages. So we'll keep talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:06.080
this. And this is part of a bigger focus that I&nbsp;
think it's important to have on workplace issues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.960
democratization of the workplace, gig economy&nbsp;
stuff. I read some interesting articles over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:21.200
weekend about different issues facing gig economy&nbsp;
workers and how tech companies that are sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.680
exhibiting predatory behavior are taking advantage&nbsp;
of them. We're going to keep talking about this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:31.440
But as a baseline, we have to first build kind&nbsp;
of the base of the pyramid. A lot of the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.800
being said by the right about the minimum wage&nbsp;
is merely disinformation, not supported by left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:43.200
wing or even many right wing economists. And&nbsp;
we'll have more about it. Send me your thoughts.&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.040
I'm on Twitter at David Pakman. We will&nbsp;
take a quick break and be back after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.680
this. It's February. It's incredibly&nbsp;
February and much to discuss today.

